/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Gears are grouped by how their shafts sit.
   - Every calculation starts at the pitch circle: the imaginary circle on which the two gears roll without slipping.
   - Tooth heights follow the module.
   - The line of action is the common tangent to the two base circles; tooth contact always lies on it.
   - At any instant two teeth touch at one point.
   - An involute is the curve traced by the end of a taut string unwound from a base circle.
   - Contact begins where the gear's addendum circle cuts the line of action and ends where the pinion's addendum circle cuts it.
*/
