/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A tooth is a short cantilever.
   - Surface hardness is usually quoted as BHN, the Brinell hardness number: a 10 mm hardened ball is pressed in with 3000 kgf and the load is divided by the dent area, so BHN is in kgf/mm².
   - The pinion rule matters more than any single number.
   - Plain carbon steels with 0.3 to 0.6 % carbon (C40, C45, 40C8) are the shop-floor default.
   - Distortion is the hidden cost of hardening.
   - Grey cast iron (FG 200, FG 260; the number is the minimum tensile strength in MPa) has flake graphite that damps vibration and breaks chips.
   - Bronze belongs to worm wheels.
*/
