/* book exam traps: Choosing the hardest steel for both wheels. A hard case needs a tough core; a tooth hardened right through is brittle and chips at the root under shock. | Reading BHN as a strength in MPa. 300 BHN suggests about 1000 MPa tensile strength for steel, but only as an estimate, and the allowable bending stress is a fraction of the strength. | Mixing hardness scales: 58 HRC is about 600 BHN. Compare pinion and gear hardness on one scale. | Pairing bronze with bronze in a worm drive, or a soft worm with a hard wheel. The wheel is the bronze member and the worm is hardened and ground. | Using cast iron for a small high-speed pinion because it runs quietly. Damping does not make up for its low tensile and shock strength. */
