/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take the worst case: one tooth carries the whole load, and the load acts at the tip, where it gives the largest bending moment at the root.
   - Here Sb is the beam strength, the largest tangential load in N the tooth can carry in bending; σb is the allowable bending stress in N/mm² (σut/3 in many Indian textbooks); b is the face width in mm; m is the module in mm; Y is the Lewis fo
   - For 20° full-depth involute teeth a common fit is Y = 0.484 − 2.87/z, which gives Y = 0.3405 at 20 teeth and 0.4362 at 60 teeth; tables differ slightly (0.322 at 20 teeth in Shigley's), so use your data book's.
   - Y grows with the number of teeth, because a small gear's teeth are thinner at the root.
   - The tangential force at the pitch circle is Ft = P/v, with P the power in W and v = πd1n_1/60 the pitch line velocity in m/s (d1 in metres, n1 in rpm).
   - Feff = Cs Ft / Cv, Sb ≥ fs Feff Feff is the effective load in N and fs the factor of safety, often 1.5.
   - Shigley and AGMA use instead a dynamic factor Kv greater than 1 that multiplies Ft; do not mix the two conventions.
   - Tooth errors e and tooth stiffness make the gears separate and strike again; Buckingham's estimate of this extra load is:
   - Fd = 21v(Ceb + Ft) / (21v + √(Ceb + Ft)) Fd is the dynamic (incremental) load in N; v is in m/s; e is the sum of the tooth errors of the pair in mm; b is the face width in mm; C is the deformation factor in N/mm², 11 400 for two steel gears
   - Surfaces fail by pitting under repeated contact stress.
*/
